# Erdős Problem #99 — effective-coefficient six-pair atomic gap `0.00335`

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Repository branch:** `research/c2-effective-sixpair-sol-20260914`  
**Claim class:** proof-only strengthening of the split-budget / iterative-pruning atomic stack  
**Dependencies:** dominant-orbit Mantel reduction; sharp three-label six-pair minimization; iterative-pruning pair theorem  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current branch had already raised the atomic train gain to `0.00234`.  The
iterative-pruning pair theorem then proved, for two atom families of masses `a,b`,

\[
\rho\ge
\min\left\{
{(a+b-K)_+^2\over10},
{2\min(a,b)^2\over29}
\right\},
\qquad K={2\over\sqrt3}.
\tag{EC.1}
\]

Its simple denominator-`10` branch is not uniform over all atom families at the
`0.003` scale if one uses only the coarse shore cap.  That does not block a global
improvement.  The small-family alternative in (EC.1) can itself be converted into a
quadratic payment in `(a+b-K)_+` using the same family cap.  The resulting uniform
coefficient is about `0.094`, after which the sharp six-pair three-label optimization
applies unchanged.

This gives

\[
\boxed{
 x\sigma\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00335,
 \qquad \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{EC.2}
\]

Thus at the same proof-only scope one may take

\[
\boxed{\eta_{grid}=0.00335.}
\tag{EC.3}
\]

This is a genuine propagation of the new iterative-pruning theorem, not decimal
retuning of the previous `0.00234` endpoint.

---

## 1. Convert the pair dichotomy to a uniform effective coefficient

Let

\[
0\le a,b\le S<K
\]

and put

\[
g=(a+b-K)_+,
\qquad
m=\min\{a,b\},
\qquad
M=\max\{a,b\}.
\]

If `g=0`, every quadratic lower bound below is trivial.  Suppose `g>0`.  Then

\[
{g\over m}
={m+M-K\over m}
=1-{K-M\over m}.
\]

For fixed `M<K`, this expression is increasing in `m`, and `m\le M`.  Therefore

\[
{g\over m}
\le2-{K\over M}
\le2-{K\over S}
=:r_S.
\tag{EC.4}
\]

Hence

\[
m\ge{g\over r_S}.
\tag{EC.5}
\]

Insert this in the second branch of (EC.1):

\[
{2m^2\over29}
\ge
{2g^2\over29r_S^2}.
\]

Consequently every such pair satisfies the single quadratic payment

\[
\boxed{
\rho\ge c_S(a+b-K)_+^2,
}
\tag{EC.6}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
 c_S:=
 \min\left\{
 {1\over10},
 {2\over29(2-K/S)^2}
 \right\}.
}
\tag{EC.7}
\]

No branch information from the iterative-pruning proof is discarded except through
this explicit worst-case ratio bound.

---

## 2. Three-label minimization with a general coefficient

The sharp SP1625 minimization was written with the earlier pair coefficient, but its
proof only needs the coefficient to be below `1/8`.

Let

\[
x=(x_1,x_2,x_3),
\qquad
 y=(y_1,y_2,y_3),
\]

with

\[
x_i,y_i\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_i x_i=\sum_i y_i=s,
\qquad
{2K\over3}<s<K.
\]

For `0<c<1/8`, define

\[
\Phi_{s,c}(x,y)
=
\sum_i x_i y_i
+c\sum_{i\ne j}(x_i+y_j-K)_+^2.
\tag{EC.8}
\]

### Lemma EC.1

For every `0<c<1/8`,

\[
\boxed{
\Phi_{s,c}(x,y)
\ge
2c\left({3s\over2}-K\right)^2.
}
\tag{EC.9}
\]

Equality is attained at, up to simultaneous relabeling and shore exchange,

\[
x=(s,0,0),
\qquad
 y=(0,s/2,s/2).
\tag{EC.10}
\]

### Proof

The SP1625 proof carries over with `c` symbolic.

Along an anti-diagonal perturbation

\[
x(t)=x+tv,
\qquad y(t)=y-tv,
\qquad \sum_i v_i=0,
\]

the same-label term has second derivative `-2||v||^2`, while the six hinge terms have
total second derivative at most `12c||v||^2`.  Since `c<1/8<1/6`, the restriction is
strictly concave.  Therefore a minimizer has at most one label positive on both shores.

If one common label remains and both shores also carry exclusive mass, use the SP1625
one-dimensional perturbation that moves the two exclusive masses oppositely.  The
same-label term again contributes `-2`, while the two moving hinge arguments contribute
at most `16c`.  Because `c<1/8`, that restriction is also strictly concave.  Hence a
minimum lies on the boundary: either the common overlap disappears, or one shore is
supported entirely on the common label.

If the supports are disjoint, one shore has support size one.  Write

\[
x=(s,0,0),
\qquad y=(0,u,s-u).
\]

Convexity gives

\[
\Phi_{s,c}
=c\bigl[(s+u-K)_+^2+(2s-u-K)_+^2\bigr]
\ge2c\left({3s\over2}-K\right)^2.
\]

If instead one shore is supported on the common label, write

\[
y=(s,0,0),
\qquad x=(a,b,s-a-b).
\]

Jensen gives

\[
\Phi_{s,c}
\ge as+2c\left({3s-a\over2}-K\right)_+^2.
\]

Put `A=3s/2-K>0`.  Where the positive part is active, the derivative in `a` is

\[
s-2c(A-a/2).
\]

Since `A<s/2` and `c<1`, this is positive.  It is also positive after the hinge turns
off.  Thus the minimum occurs at `a=0`, giving exactly the right side of (EC.9).
This proves the lemma. `square`

---

## 3. Dominant-orbit constants at `eta=0.00335`

Assume for contradiction that a Type-II train-measure limit satisfies

\[
A:=x\sigma\le\bar A:=2s_2+\eta,
\qquad
s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\eta:=0.00335.
\tag{EC.11}
\]

Use the existing dominant-orbit reduction.  The dominant projective three-cycle has
mass at least

\[
M_0={4s_2^2\over\bar A},
\tag{EC.12}
\]

with outside mass at most

\[
R_0=\bar A-M_0,
\]

and conservative Mantel deficit

\[
d_0={\bar A^2\over4}-s_2^2+{R_0^2\over4}.
\tag{EC.13}
\]

The maximum-degree Mantel partition gives two dominant-orbit shore masses each at
least

\[
\boxed{
s_0={M_0\over2}-\sqrt{d_0},
}
\tag{EC.14}
\]

and total missing cross-shore pair mass at most `(2d_0+o(1))D^2`.

As in LA90/SP1625, trim inside the three atom windows until both retained shores have
normalized mass exactly `s_0`.  Write the retained atom masses as `x_i,y_i`.
Trimming cannot create missing pairs.

Every retained atom family is bounded by its original Mantel shore.  The standard
shore-imbalance estimate therefore gives the safe family cap

\[
\boxed{
S_0:={\bar A\over2}+\sqrt{d_0}.
}
\tag{EC.15}
\]

The exact certificate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\bar A&=1.907975613727914\ldots,\\
M_0&=1.901281495616508\ldots,\\
R_0&=0.006694118111406\ldots,\\
d_0&=0.00320425633231662\ldots,\\
s_0&=0.894034596796932\ldots,\\
S_0&=1.010593957875280\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{EC.16}
\]

In particular

\[
{2K\over3}<s_0<S_0<K.
\tag{EC.17}
\]

For this cap,

\[
r_0:=2-{K\over S_0}
=0.857404074721614\ldots,
\tag{EC.18}
\]

so the effective coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
 c_0={2\over29r_0^2}
=0.0938125436217642\ldots< {1\over10}<{1\over8}.
}
\tag{EC.19}
\]

Thus every distinct-label block satisfies

\[
\rho_{ij}\ge c_0(x_i+y_j-K)_+^2.
\tag{EC.20}
\]

---

## 4. All nine label blocks contradict the missing budget

Same-label atom pairs do not cross, so they are all missing.  The nine label blocks are
disjoint.  Therefore the global Mantel missing budget gives

\[
2d_0
\ge
\sum_i x_i y_i
+c_0\sum_{i\ne j}(x_i+y_j-K)_+^2.
\tag{EC.21}
\]

Apply Lemma EC.1 with `s=s_0` and `c=c_0`:

\[
2d_0
\ge
2c_0\left({3s_0\over2}-K\right)^2.
\tag{EC.22}
\]

Equivalently every survivor under (EC.11) would require

\[
\boxed{
d_0\ge
c_0\left({3s_0\over2}-K\right)^2.
}
\tag{EC.23}
\]

But exact interval arithmetic gives

\[
{3s_0\over2}-K
=0.186351356816147\ldots
\]

and

\[
c_0\left({3s_0\over2}-K\right)^2
=0.00325781208415897\ldots,
\]

while

\[
d_0=0.00320425633231662\ldots .
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
 c_0\left({3s_0\over2}-K\right)^2-d_0
>5.35\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{EC.24}
\]

Contradiction.  Hence (EC.2) holds.

The exact certificate is

`research/verify_target_c_effective_six_pair_atomic_gap_00335.py`.

---

## 5. Method ceiling and next target

For this particular effective-coefficient relaxation, the scalar equality is near

\[
\eta=0.00338555.
\]

So `0.00335` deliberately retains a visible margin.  This is **not** asserted to be
the ceiling of the full iterative-pruning pair theorem.  The reduction from (EC.1) to
one coefficient `c_S` is worst-case and can lose information about which of the six
pairs enters the small-family branch.

The next atomic improvement, if useful, should therefore optimize the six pairwise
`min{g^2/10,2m^2/29}` payments jointly instead of replacing all six by the same
worst-case coefficient.

More importantly, the outer-tooth/long-side and shallow-tooth geometry on current
`main` is now likely to be a better closing route than recovering another few
`10^-5` from this scalar endpoint.

---

## 6. Scope audit

- The theorem has the same full-core Type-II train-measure scope as SP1625 and the
  split-budget `0.00234` theorem.
- No majority assignment is used.
- Trimming only removes retained trains and cannot create missing pairs.
- Same-label pairs are charged as actual missing blocks; distinct-label pairs use the
  iterative-pruning theorem.
- The family cap `S_0` is a safe Mantel-shore cap, not an assumed equal split.
- Lemma EC.1 is the existing sharp three-label minimization with its coefficient made
  explicit; `c_0<1/8` is exactly what the concavity proof needs.
- No source point is deleted from the global source, no KKT coefficient floor is used,
  and no exact unit equilateral triangle or strict competitor is claimed.

**Disposition:** proof-only strengthening.  C-II and Level I remain open.
